# Environment Configuration - Security Best Practices

## Overview
This document explains how to properly manage sensitive environment variables for security.

---

## ❌ WHAT NOT TO DO (Security Anti-Patterns)

```javascript
// DON'T: Hardcoded secrets
const DB_PASSWORD = "admin123";
const API_KEY = "sk-live-xyz";

// DON'T: Commit .env files with real secrets
// .env files should NEVER be committed

// DON'T: Store secrets in source code
const config = {
  db: "postgres://admin:password@localhost/db"
};
```

---

## ✅ WHAT TO DO (Secure Patterns)

### 1. Use Environment Variables

```javascript
// app.js
require('dotenv').config();

const config = {
  database: {
    url: process.env.DATABASE_URL,
    connectionTimeout: parseInt(process.env.DB_TIMEOUT) || 5000
  },
  api: {
    key: process.env.API_KEY,
    secret: process.env.API_SECRET
  },
  server: {
    port: parseInt(process.env.PORT) || 3000,
    environment: process.env.NODE_ENV || 'development'
  }
};

// Validate required variables
const requiredVars = ['DATABASE_URL', 'API_KEY'];
requiredVars.forEach(varName => {
  if (!process.env[varName]) {
    throw new Error(`Missing required environment variable: ${varName}`);
  }
});

module.exports = config;
```

### 2. Create .env.example (NO SECRETS)

```env
# Database
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/mydb
DB_TIMEOUT=5000

# API Keys (use dummy values for examples)
API_KEY=your_api_key_here
API_SECRET=your_api_secret_here

# Server
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=development

# Logging
LOG_LEVEL=info
```

### 3. Add to .gitignore

```
# .gitignore
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
.env.test
```

### 4. Configuration by Environment

#### Development (.env.local - LOCAL MACHINE ONLY)
```env
NODE_ENV=development
DATABASE_URL=postgres://dev_user:dev_pass@localhost:5432/dev_db
API_KEY=dev_key_123
LOG_LEVEL=debug
```

#### Staging (Use CI/CD secrets)
- Set in: GitHub Actions / GitLab CI / Jenkins
- NEVER commit to repository
- Use secure secret management: AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault

#### Production (Use CI/CD secrets)
- Set in: GitHub Actions / GitLab CI / Jenkins
- NEVER commit to repository
- Use secure secret management: AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault

---

## 🔐 Secret Management Solutions

### Option 1: Environment Variables in CI/CD

#### GitHub Actions
```yaml
# .github/workflows/deploy.yml
name: Deploy

on: [push]

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v2
      - name: Deploy
        env:
          DATABASE_URL: ${{ secrets.DATABASE_URL }}
          API_KEY: ${{ secrets.API_KEY }}
          STRIPE_SECRET: ${{ secrets.STRIPE_SECRET }}
        run: npm start
```

#### GitLab CI
```yaml
# .gitlab-ci.yml
deploy:
  stage: deploy
  environment:
    name: production
  variables:
    DATABASE_URL: $DATABASE_URL  # Set in GitLab admin
    API_KEY: $API_KEY
  script:
    - npm start
```

### Option 2: AWS Secrets Manager

```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const secretsManager = new AWS.SecretsManager();

async function getSecrets() {
  try {
    const data = await secretsManager.getSecretValue({
      SecretId: 'prod/myapp/secrets'
    }).promise();

    const secrets = JSON.parse(data.SecretString);
    return secrets;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Failed to get secrets:', error);
    throw error;
  }
}

module.exports = { getSecrets };
```

### Option 3: HashiCorp Vault

```javascript
const vaultClient = require('node-vault')({
  endpoint: process.env.VAULT_ADDR,
  token: process.env.VAULT_TOKEN
});

async function getSecrets() {
  const secret = await vaultClient.read('secret/data/myapp');
  return secret.data.data;
}

module.exports = { getSecrets };
```

### Option 4: Docker Secrets (for Docker Swarm)

```dockerfile
# Dockerfile
FROM node:16
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN npm install
CMD ["node", "app.js"]
```

```bash
# Use Docker secrets
docker secret create db_password ./db.pass
docker service create \
  --secret db_password \
  --env DATABASE_URL=/run/secrets/db_password \
  my-app:latest
```

---

## 📋 Configuration Validation Example

```javascript
// config/validator.js
function validateConfig() {
  const required = {
    DATABASE_URL: 'Database connection string',
    API_KEY: 'API key for external service',
    NODE_ENV: 'Environment (development/staging/production)',
    PORT: 'Server port',
  };

  const errors = [];

  for (const [key, description] of Object.entries(required)) {
    if (!process.env[key]) {
      errors.push(`Missing: ${key} - ${description}`);
    }
  }

  if (errors.length > 0) {
    console.error('Configuration validation failed:');
    errors.forEach(error => console.error(`  - ${error}`));
    process.exit(1);
  }

  console.log('✓ Configuration validation passed');
}

module.exports = { validateConfig };
```

---

## 🔄 Credential Rotation

### Best Practices

1. **Automated Rotation**
   - Use AWS Secrets Manager with automatic rotation
   - Set rotation frequency (30-90 days recommended)

2. **Manual Rotation Checklist**
   ```
   [ ] Generate new credentials
   [ ] Update in secret management system
   [ ] Test with new credentials
   [ ] Monitor for errors
   [ ] Revoke old credentials
   [ ] Document change
   ```

3. **Zero-Downtime Rotation**
   ```javascript
   // Support both old and new secrets during transition
   const secrets = {
     current: process.env.API_KEY,
     previous: process.env.API_KEY_OLD // Used temporarily
   };
   ```

---

## 📊 Security Monitoring

### Log sensitive access (but not the values!)

```javascript
const logger = require('pino')();

function loadSecret(secretName) {
  logger.info(`Loading secret: ${secretName}`);
  // Load secret...
  logger.info(`Secret loaded successfully: ${secretName}`);
  // DON'T log the actual value!
}
```

### Alert on failed secret loads

```javascript
async function loadSecretWithAlert() {
  try {
    const secret = await getSecret();
    return secret;
  } catch (error) {
    logger.error(`SECURITY ALERT: Failed to load secrets`);
    // Send alert to monitoring system
    await alertOps('Secret retrieval failed');
    throw error;
  }
}
```

---

## 🚀 Quick Start Checklist

```bash
# 1. Create .env.example (with dummy values)
cp .env .env.example
# Edit .env.example to remove real secrets

# 2. Add .env to .gitignore
echo ".env" >> .gitignore

# 3. Install dotenv
npm install dotenv

# 4. Load in app
require('dotenv').config();

# 5. Use in code
const db = process.env.DATABASE_URL;

# 6. For production, set in CI/CD or secret manager
# Don't use .env files
```

---

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid

| Mistake | Impact | Fix |
|---------|--------|-----|
| Committing .env files | Permanent exposure in git history | Add to .gitignore, use filter-repo |
| Logging secrets | Secrets in log files | Use sanitize middleware |
| Using same secrets everywhere | Single point of failure | Use environment-specific secrets |
| Not rotating credentials | Long-lived compromised keys | Rotate every 30-90 days |
| Hardcoding secrets | Easy to find in source code | Always use environment variables |
| Storing secrets in code comments | Easy to find | Never, ever do this |

---

## 🔗 References

- [Node.js Security - Environment Variables](https://nodejs.org/en/knowledge/file-system/security/introduction/)
- [OWASP - Secrets Management](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html)
- [12-Factor App - Config](https://12factor.net/config)
- [AWS Secrets Manager](https://aws.amazon.com/secrets-manager/)
- [HashiCorp Vault](https://www.vaultproject.io/)

